<Layout title="About & Methodology — 7 Deadly Sins Test" description="See every IPIP item, scoring direction, and limitation behind our 7 deadly sins test. Public-domain questions with a transparent entertainment interpretation.">
<article class="article-page"><a class="back-home" href="/">← Back to the test</a><h1>Real items.<br/>Our interpretation.</h1><p class="lead">Seven Sins is a free personality-themed entertainment site. We use public-domain IPIP statements and map the answers to seven familiar themes. The mapping is ours; it is not an official IPIP seven-sin scale.</p>
<h2>Where the questions come from</h2><p>The <a href="https://ipip.ori.org/">International Personality Item Pool</a> is a collection of personality items and scales. Its <a href="https://ipip.ori.org/newPermission.htm">permission page</a> explicitly allows anyone to use IPIP materials for commercial or non-commercial purposes without applying for permission.</p><p>All 35 statements below were checked against the <a href="https://ipip.ori.org/AlphabeticalItemList.htm">alphabetical list of 3,320 IPIP items</a>. We preserve the original English wording, with line breaks normalized. The identifiers are the survey and item codes supplied by IPIP. Some items also appear in the <a href="https://ipip.ori.org/newNEOKey.htm">IPIP scales measuring constructs similar to NEO personality facets</a>.</p>
<h2>How scores are calculated</h2><p>Each statement has five answers: Very inaccurate (0), Moderately inaccurate (1), Neither accurate nor inaccurate (2), Moderately accurate (3), and Very accurate (4). For reverse-scored items, we use 4 minus the answer value. Each theme has five equally weighted items.</p><p><strong>Theme score = sum of the five keyed values ÷ 20 × 100.</strong> Scores range from 0% to 100% in five-point steps. All 35 answers are required, and we do not silently count missing answers as neutral.</p><p>The percentages are independent score proportions, not population percentiles, probabilities, or the proportion of your personality made of each sin. The seven scores need not sum to 100%. The maximum determines the strongest theme; ties remain ties. In the image, the colored region extends from the center in proportion to the score along its sector’s radius. The printed percentage is the score; filled area is not a percentile.</p><p>The wording of each explanation varies across low (0–30%), middle (35–60%), and high (65–100%) scores. Those are editorial bands for readable explanations, not validated clinical cutoffs. The question set and scoring are version 1; shared result links preserve that version and the seven scores.</p>
<h2>What the mapping can—and cannot—say</h2><p class="notice">This particular selection, category mapping, and interpretation have not been validated as a psychological instrument. The validity of another IPIP scale does not transfer to our selection. This test cannot diagnose a condition, assess moral worth, or establish that a person is “sinful.”</p><ul>{SINS.map(s=><li><strong>{s.name}:</strong> {s.description}</li>)}</ul><p>In particular, jealousy is not identical to envy; sexual desire is not misconduct; ambition is not automatically greed; eating behavior is not a diagnosis; and difficulty beginning tasks can have many causes. Five items per theme give a limited snapshot, and your context, interpretation, and willingness to answer honestly can change the result. The categories come from the cultural seven-sins framing, not from an IPIP endorsement.</p>
<h2>The complete item key</h2><p>“Forward” means a more accurate answer increases the theme score. “Reverse” means it decreases it. This table is provided for transparency; reading it before answering may influence your responses.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption class="sr-only">All 35 original IPIP statements, source item codes, assigned themes, and score directions</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Theme</th><th scope="col">Original IPIP statement</th><th scope="col">IPIP code</th><th scope="col">Key</th></tr></thead><tbody>{questions.map(q=><tr><td>{SINS.find(s=>s.id===q.sin)!.name}</td><td>{q.text}</td><td>{q.ipipId}</td><td>{q.reverse?'Reverse':'Forward'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>About this site</h2><p>Seven Sins is an independent entertainment website. It is not affiliated with IPIP, the Oregon Research Institute, or another test publisher. The seven-sin mapping and result explanations were created for this site. Questions are public-domain IPIP material.</p><p>Your answers and image are processed in the browser. See <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> for local storage, hosting, and shared result links, or <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> for intended use. You can report an issue through the source repository linked below.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/toolution/seven-deadly-sins-test/issues">Report an issue</a> · Source items checked November 19, 2026.</p><a class="primary-button" href="/">Take the test →</a></article>
</Layout>
